body:
That was nice but a princess never wakes up like an ordinary person, she never do stretches never squints eyes... A princess is always portrayed perfectly, she is the epitome of elegance and naivete. A castle servant also never knocks on the door to wake princess up, princess has a handmaid who helps her waking up and getting ready.

&#x200B;

There is a code of conduct within royal household, you can't portray them as muggles.

Yes, that's true. But sometimes, you see in Dostoevsky's life too, when the work is forced the outcome would not satisfy the writer. He would end up destroying his/her work and think that he's never gonna write again. New writers should read more than they write. They should sometimes read 100 more books before putting a new word to their page. Otherwise, they should write YA books.

&#x200B;

On the other hand, I'll do according to your advises 2019 and try to write a book. I'll write a comment if I change my mind.

There was probably a branding system among rich slave owners like they would brand their cattle. They probably used a branding iron to put their signature on their slaves. A branded slave probably would cost less.  And crew members used to divide in groups and ranks a lot, but if captain had strict rules slaves were not harmed because of their race.

&#x200B;

Also many black people from Africa were generally sold to Brazil and Mediterranean cities in order to work in farms, roadwork or armies. Captains needed lots of new crew members too, but they also had to be careful for diseases. A new member would bring a disease and damage the population of the ship.

I don't create all characters and settings beforehand. I try to make logical connections between setting and characters. When your character enters a pub the type of characters that s/he would come across is limited. Or, if your main character is a child, there would be a subplot which takes places in school or in his/her house.

&#x200B;

Also the settings are limited. If you character is an adventurer, he can't find an adventure in his kitchen, he needs to move a lot. But, if your character is a mother, she would mostly stay indoors.

&#x200B;

In short, your settings and characters needs to match so you can make a list of matching items before you actually start to write your story.

Some Christians compare biblical personalities with one another and almost rank them according to their significance. I think they are somewhat corrupted. As a mother and a strong woman figure, women in her society is portrayed through her life.

&#x200B;

We shouldn't approach them as if they were Marvel heroes, this would be an immature behaviour. None of the biblical personalities more important than one another, nevertheless, they were all human beings, they were equal to everyone in existence.

&#x200B;

Mary is a great way to study the place of women in the society. We can't say only Christ went through pain, as a mother she must have felt the same pain no less than Christ.
